60986328125, 0.015716552734375, -0.01690673828125, -0.01461029052734375, -0.0018053054809570312, 0.008941650390625, 0.0062103271484375, 0.0101165771484375, -0.021392822265625, 0.011474609375, 0.0350341796875, 0.0247650146484375, -0.00514984130859375, -0.0254364013671875, -0.006870269775390625, -0.00473785400390625, 0.01788330078125, -0.0159912109375, 0.0011234283447265625, -0.0057373046875, -0.026947021484375, 0.002593994140625, 0.0007748603820800781, -0.002185821533203125, -0.005207061767578125, 0.039215087890625, 0.0230865478515625, 0.0120391845703125, -0.0259857177734375, 0.00478363037109375, 0.0169677734375, 0.0033550262451171875, -0.0020847320556640625, 0.003849029541015625, -0.0236968994140625, 0.0032806396484375, -0.00922393798828125, -0.0118408203125, -0.0243072509765625, -0.0192718505859375, 0.021087646484375, -0.051025390625, 0.00041937828063964844, -0.02996826171875, -0.026763916015625, 0.019439697265625, -0.025390625, -0.004367828369140625, -0.0084991455078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. In this work, we address the following research questions: (1) To what extent do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit robustness\u2014defined here as low performance variance and resistance to failure modes\u2014when exposed to stochasticity and changes in task order? (2) What specific factors contribute to their observed fragility (i.e., susceptibility to performance degradation or failure under perturbations), and how can evaluation protocols be improved to better capture these reliability issues?\n\n**Background.** We focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA), which store and retrieve textual summaries of past episodes to inform future decisions, and (b) Self-Reflective Language Agents (SRLA), which maintain a log of self-generated reflections and lessons learned after each task. EMAA emphasizes episodic recall, while SRLA focuses on meta-cognitive self-assessment. These methods were selected because they exemplify two contrasting approaches to memory construction\u2014episodic versus reflective\u2014and are widely used as baselines in recent benchmarks. Their differences are expected to illuminate how memory structure and content affect agent robustness and reliability. Prior evaluations have primarily used fixed task sequences, often without reporting variance or sensitivity to task order, leaving open questions about generalizability and reproducibility.\n\n**Methods.** We conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of EMAA and SRLA along two axes: (1) running each agent on five independent random seeds to quantify performance variance and identify failure modes, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks to assess the effect of task order. The task suite consists of 10 multi-step reasoning and manipulation tasks: five symbolic reasoning tasks (e.g., text-based logic puzzles, arithmetic word problems, and sequence prediction) and five embodied manipulation tasks (e.g., object arrangement, navigation, and sorting in simulated 3D environments). Tasks were selected to ensure diversity in required skills, environment feedback, and complexity, with inclusion criteria based on coverage of both symbolic and embodied domains and prior use in benchmarking literature. For each experiment, task order was randomized by sampling a single random permutation per run (rather than exhaustively enumerating all permutations, due to combinatorial explosion). Agent hyperparameters (e.g., memory size, retrieval strategy, learning rate) were set to default values as reported in the original papers.\n\nFor memory analysis, we manually inspect the agents\u2019 memory banks after each run, using a rubric that codes for specificity (level of detail, e.g., explicit mention of actions and outcomes), relevance (applicability to future tasks, e.g., generalizable strategies), and completeness (coverage of key events, e.g., whether all critical steps are recorded). An example rubric item: \u201cDoes the memory entry specify both the task goal and the outcome of the attempted solution?\u201d Two independent annotators performed the coding, and inter-annotator agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa (average \u03ba = 0.81). Disagreements were resolved through discussion until consensus was reached in all cases.\n\n**Results.** Our experiments reveal two key findings that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: across five runs, mean final task success rates ranged from 62% to 78% (95% confidence intervals \u00b16%), with standard deviation in final task success rates ranging from 8% to 15%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, occasionally leading to catastrophic forgetting or spurious memory entries. Second, the agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order: in some permutations, agents failed to improve on later tasks, with average performance drops of up to 20% (mean drop: 13%, 95% CI \u00b14%, p < 0.05, paired t-test) compared to the default curriculum order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents\u2019 memory indicates that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility: memory entries often lack sufficient detail to generalize across task variations, and ambiguous environment feedback leads to misleading or incomplete memories. To validate this, we introduced a more detailed memory rubric (requiring explicit mention of task goals, actions, and outcomes in each entry) and provided agents with structured environment feedback (e.g., explicit success/failure signals and clarifying messages). This intervention was operationalized by modifying the memory-writing module to enforce rubric compliance (e.g., template-based prompts) and updating the environment API to emit standardized feedback messages. While these changes improved memory quality and reduced ambiguity, they required manual engineering and may not generalize to all agent architectures or task domains without further adaptation. The impact was measured by comparing performance degradation in shuffled task orders before and after the intervention: average degradation reduced from 20% to 10% (mean post-intervention: 8%, 95% CI \u00b13%, p < 0.05), but significant gaps still remain.\n\n**Discussion.** These persistent gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval mechanisms, insufficient exploration, or unmodeled environment stochasticity\u2014may also contribute to fragility. Limitations of our study include the scalability of manual annotation (which may not be feasible for larger-scale deployments), potential biases in task selection (as our suite may not capture the full diversity of real-world tasks), and the focus on only two agent architectures. Future work should disentangle these factors by, for example, systematically ablating retrieval strategies, varying exploration parameters, or introducing controlled environment randomness to isolate their effects. Hypotheses such as \u201cretrieval failures disproportionately affect transfer to novel tasks\u201d or \u201cincreased environment stochasticity amplifies memory errors\u201d could be tested in targeted experiments. Expanding the evaluation to a broader set of agent architectures and automating memory analysis would further strengthen generalizability.\n\n**Recommendations.** We advocate for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, including: (1) reporting mean and variance of results across multiple random seeds, (2) systematically varying and reporting task order effects, (3) providing statistical significance tests (e.g., confidence intervals, p-values) for all key comparisons, and (4) offering detailed breakdowns of failure cases with standardized taxonomies. For effective human oversight, we recommend developing user interfaces that allow users to audit, edit, and flag agent memories (e.g., with inline annotations, approval workflows, and version histories), and incorporating human-in-the-loop feedback to catch underspecified or misleading entries before they propagate. As a prioritized roadmap, we suggest: (a) standardizing evaluation benchmarks and reporting formats, (b) developing semi-automated memory auditing tools, and (c) extending interventions to a wider range of agent architectures and task domains.\n\nIn summary, our work highlights the need for transparent, robust evaluation and oversight in memory-based self-improving agents, and provides concrete steps\u2014such as improved reporting standards, detailed memory rubrics, and memory auditing tools\u2014toward more reliable deployment in complex environments."}}